  "meta": {
    "title": "The Clearing House",
    "slug": "the-clearing-house",
    "archetype": "HIDDEN_MECHANISM"
  },
  "narration": "Modern finance is a game of trust. But trust is a friction. The real engine of wealth isn't the trade. It is the clearing. In 1853, fifty-two banks in New York were drowning in their own gold. Porters walked miles daily just to settle individual debts between branches. It was slow. It was dangerous. It was an 18th-century bottleneck. Then came the Clearing House. A single room where all debts met. They didn't move the gold. They just moved the numbers. By netting the totals, they reduced physical gold movement by ninety-five percent. Efficiency created the first true network effect in American banking. Today, this 'invisible plumbing' settles two trillion dollars every single day. The winner isn't the one with the most gold. It\u2019s the one who controls the ledger.",
  "bgm": {
    "trackId": "bgm_dark_high_drone_01"
  },
